A||||2||88FORMULA-T-TOP-GUY||Need advice on half shaft replacement.||||1||
Z||000000||88FORMULA-T-TOP-GUY||06-02-2001||02:01 AM||||My inner boot is split and I have been told by numerous people that since I have 99,000 miles on my car I might as well replace the half shaft instead of just the boot. My manual doesn't cover the 88 and the instructions for the 87 and earlier don't seem to apply. Is it a lot of work? I can get it done for $100 labor. Should I pay or do it myself?  Thanks.  <BR>p.s. - is it true that split boots are only made for the outer?||||reg||1
Z||000001||isthiswhereiputausername?||06-02-2001||08:50 AM||||YOu should be able to fix it yourself, you would need the crimping tool to attach the clamp to hold the boot on. <P>also replacing depends on how long it has ben broken.. Did it have a chance to wear from dirt/dust in there? any noises in it?  also when your putting it back on, make sure you grease it good or you will be replacing it again.<P>You should have a look at it and see how bad its ripped and to decide if you want to attempt the project yourself..<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||EdsB52||06-02-2001||12:21 PM||||If the boot hasn't been ripped for long and it still has a little grease inside, I would just replace the boot. CV joints probably last 250,000 miles or more if kept greased, the problem is that they lose grease and go soon after. If you go the boot route, don't buy a split boot or a "one size fits all" boot at AutoZone. Buy one from NAPA or some other reputable place.<P>I would just change it myself. Be sure to scribe on the spindle, where the strut interfaces. If you don't, you'll have to get it realigned. Another thing to do is break the axle nut loose before you remove the wheel or anything. Also, be sure to drop in new output shaft seals (2) in the tranny. You'll need a special cup driver to install them, but you'll definately want to.||||reg||1
